Return every source_id exactly once. When at least three candidates are supplied,
set selected=true for exactly three roles; otherwise select every supplied role.
Selection is based on target fit, evidence strength,
technical depth, recency, and non-redundant coverage. Do not rewrite bullets.

How to use JD_REQUIREMENT_MAP (when present):
- Each requirement has an `id` (R1, R2, ...) and a `status`: `direct` = the
  master states it; `proxy` = the master lacks it but `proxy_fact_ids` hold the
  closest real evidence (named by `proxy_terms`); `missing` = no evidence.
- `covered_requirement_ids` lists every requirement ID this candidate's own
  facts evidence, directly or through a listed proxy fact. Use only IDs from the
  map. Code combines these IDs with relevance to pick the final set, so a
  complementary set that covers more weighted requirements beats several
  entries proving the same one.
- A role whose title matches the target job (see `role` and `title_aliases`)
  is strong evidence on its own: keep title-matching roles. Proxy facts that
  live in other roles or projects do not require selecting those entries; the
  evidence planner may move them into a selected role's bullets.

`relevance` anchors (integer 0-100): 90+ = title and evidence match the target
role and cover a must-have; 70-89 = strong evidence for several requirements;
40-69 = partial or supporting evidence; below 40 = little bearing on this job.

Return ONLY:
{
  "candidates": [
    {
      "source_id": "work[0]",
      "relevance": 0,
      "selected": true,
      "matched_requirements": ["supported JD requirement"],
      "covered_requirement_ids": ["R1"],
      "reason": "short evidence-based reason"
    }
  ]
}
